    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_context` in `app/context.py`, and all 7 tests pass (the 4 existing ones plus 3 new ones).\n\n```python\ndocs = retriever.invoke(question, config={\"tags\": [TRACE_TAG]})\n```\n\n- **Lookup:** it calls `retriever.invoke(...)`, which every LangChain retriever provides, so it works with both `KeywordRetriever` and a vector-store retriever. I used `invoke` rather than the older `get_relevant_documents`, which isn't in this version of langchain-core.\n- **Trace tag:** `TRACE_TAG` is passed in the call's config, so the lookup run carries it.\n- **Output:** it follows your spec:\n  - a document is skipped if its `source` has already been used;\n  - it stops after `MAX_SOURCES` entries, keeping the retriever's order;\n  - each entry is `[n] <source>` followed by the document text with surrounding whitespace removed;\n  - entries are separated by one blank line;\n  - it returns `\"\"` when nothing is found.\n\nNo dependencies were added.\n\n**New tests (`tests/test_context.py`):** they cover skipping repeated sources, the limit and the formatting together, the empty result, and the trace tag. The tag test uses a small `KeywordRetriever` subclass that records the tags on each lookup. My first version of that test failed because it attached a callback to the retriever itself, and `invoke` only uses callbacks passed in the call's config. Only that test was wrong; `build_context` itself didn't change."
  },
